WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Run your MATLAB workflow with native ISSM, then retain its results.
Why: Bring your source, referenced data and native outputs into one reviewed workflow.
Look for: Use a verified BYO AWS connection, your MATLAB license and Connector.

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Download the native tutorial packet and extract four input files.
Why: The entry script, two helpers and JSON must be available before importing.
Look for: Scripts: runme.m, Launch.par, validate_launch_dataset.m; data: issm-shelf-small-v1.json.

3
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Name your owned example, then choose New example.
Why: An owned example gives your imported scripts a separate editable home.
Look for: Keep that example selected for scripts and dataset references.

4
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Choose Upload to store the selected JSON dataset.
Why: Uploading stores the selected JSON in your workspace dataset library.
Look for: Expected: Uploaded: issm-shelf-small-v1.json.

5
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Read the uploaded filename and select it in the dataset list.
Why: The filename confirms which dataset will be attached next.
Look for: The selected-file count changes from 1 to 0 when upload finishes.

6
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Choose Reference in example to attach the selected JSON.
Why: A reference binds this dataset to the current owned example.
Look for: The next staged run will receive this dataset.

7
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Read the reference confirmation: data/issm-shelf-small-v1.json.
Why: The exact relative path connects the attachment to the entry script.
Look for: This exact relative path is read by runme.m.

8
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Select all three scripts; enable Replace, then Import script files.
Why: Replace is essential because a new ISSM example includes a starter runme.m.
Look for: Check count 0 → 3 → 0; replacement updates the starter runme.m.

9
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Confirm all three filenames and Files are saved.
Why: Check saved source before the next staging step snapshots it.
Look for: The accepted entry script is 37 lines; every submitted input hash matched.

10
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Confirm Action Run and Run target runme.m.
Why: The entrypoint chooses which MATLAB workflow is actually executed.
Look for: Launch.par, the validator and referenced JSON accompany the entrypoint.

11
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:33.000
Review the owned ISSM run, then launch from the bottom of the review.
Why: The normal review checks resources, license and the selected example.
Look for: Check the model, example, resources, configured license and Connector.

12
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:43.000
Follow the new submission until Completed.
Why: Follow the new submission rather than a previous completed run.
Look for: Wait for this run’s results, rather than a previous completed run.

13
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Check completion before opening the native results.
Why: Startup, task time and script time describe different intervals.
Look for: Startup 219.980 s; Batch task 45.010 s; script interval 2.156 s.

14
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:13.000
Choose StressbalanceSolution → Vel, then Render.
Why: Render displays the selected native result without rerunning the solve.
Look for: Expected: one diagnostic solution with 13 native nodal values.

15
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Inspect the saved native velocity figure and its units.
Why: Use the figure, mesh count and node-weighted metric together.
Look for: Speed: 0–18.89046 m/year; node-RMS: 6.2386984 m/year.

16
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:52.000
Choose Download results, then save the fresh archive link.
Why: Retain the native fields with source/data hashes and runtime provenance.
Look for: Retain the 13-member result ZIP with the input and runtime manifests.

17
00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Interpret the script’s actual initial and boundary conditions.
Why: The actual script and boundary conditions define this diagnostic result.
Look for: Zero initial and constrained-boundary velocity; the JSON speed is unused.
